Z||000001||FieroJoe||01-02-2001||01:03 PM||||If those gauges didnt have the exact same electrical connections, you'd have a heck of a time getting all the sending units, ECM, and VSS to work with the new ones.  The oil, fuel, and temp gauges on Fiero's work with certain differences in resistances put out by the sending units to display the infomation, and there is nothing to say that the ones you want to put in would be the same, to give you enough accuracy.<BR>    As for the casing that they come in, and all the physical aspects of the gauges, im sure there could be a way to put them in.<BR>   but the electrical is the main thing to think about.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||01-02-2001||01:13 PM||||I guess this doesn't directly answer your question, but it might help. I just replaced my 85MPH speedometer/odometer/trip-odometer unit with a digital unit from Intellogix that I purchased from Summit Engineering. Following the included directions, I conneccted the input wire to one of the two wires coming from the vehicle speed sensor, and I connected the ground wire to the engine block. It came set up for 8000 pulses per mile and I calibrated it to accept 4000 pulses per mile. I kept the speedometer printed circuit board since it supplies signals to the cruise control and to the ECM.<P>I guess at least part of your problem will be to try to do what I described above for the speedometer portion of your new unit.<P>I hope this helps - good luck||||reg||1
